const meanings=[
 'Recognize legacy BIOS firmware startup context and the transition to UEFI; no precise cryptography assessment is proposed.',
 'Recognize certificate textual encoding with boundary labels and base64; retain format context without implying key protection or trust.',
 'Recognize the intended later-long-term-key-compromise property; withhold precise scope/link until fresh ephemeral/session-secret premises are explicit.',
 'Recognize distinct S/MIME message signing and recipient encryption capabilities, with narrow email-control vocabulary support only.',
 'Recognize two explicitly different SAN senses; withhold the shared identity precise link until certificate naming and storage-network meanings are disambiguated.',
 'Recognize vault/reference substitution context; withhold precise scope/link until token sensitivity and implementation limits are qualified.',
 'Recognize platform cryptographic-module key custody and measurement context; narrow 1.4 vocabulary support only, not executed policy or trust validation.'
];
const qualifications=[
 'HOLD BIOS as all modern firmware, UEFI equivalence, Secure Boot enforcement or automatic cryptographic trust. Legacy startup firmware context does not assess encryption, certificate validation or key custody. A firmware password is not removed-drive encryption; preserve prior m159 distinction. UEFI replaces the older interface while firmware implementation and enabled security features vary. Context4.1 is agent-inferred firmware-hardening vocabulary, not complete assessment. No firmware setting, boot chain or device was inspected.',
 'HOLD base64 and PEM labels as encryption, safe private-key storage, authentic issuer or complete certificate/path/name validation. RFC7468 textual encodings cover more than certificates, including public keys, requests, CRLs and encrypted or unencrypted private-key structures; the current entry teaches only a certificate-format subset. Labels/containers do not independently establish encrypted contents, parsing safety or trusted keys. Preserve prior PEM-card context-only scope. No certificate/file was parsed and no key was handled.',
 'HOLD past sessions remain protected as unconditional after any compromise. Intended forward secrecy needs suitable authenticated fresh ephemeral key establishment and secret handling/erasure; later long-term authentication-key theft alone must not recover earlier session keys. Session-secret theft, retained ephemeral secrets, endpoint/plaintext compromise, future authentication and PSK-only/0-RTT modes have separate limits. A certificate, cipher name, rekey or fresh-looking key alone is insufficient. Preserve prior qualified PFS-card and question premises without withdrawing their pending dispositions. No protocol or capture was tested.',
 'HOLD adding encryption and signatures as both always applied, public-key encryption of every content byte, transport encryption or human/content trust. S/MIME may sign, encrypt or combine them; hybrid recipient encryption protects a content-encryption key separately from bulk content. Identity binding, certificate validation, signing/recipient key custody and verification assumptions remain. Transport TLS/SMTP, DKIM and DMARC are distinct controls. Metadata and compromised endpoints remain exposed. Existing support-node objective codes are empty; no active schema edit. No message or signature was processed.',
 'HOLD SAN as one mastery identity, certificate-only expansion or full certificate acceptance. Subject Alternative Name supplies typed identifiers; application reference-identifier matching is separate from path/time/revocation/key-usage validation. Modern TLS uses relevant SAN identifiers, not simply all extra names; its wildcard rule covers one complete leftmost label, not apex or arbitrary deeper names. Dedicated block-storage networking is an unrelated explicit sense and gains no cryptographic scope. No new split node or union is approved. Preserve prior m221/m238 certificate premises; no certificate or storage device was tested.',
 'HOLD non-sensitive token as universal safety, anonymity, compliance or no authorization value. Tokens, mappings and linkages may remain sensitive or usable within their system; exposure and reversibility depend on design and access controls. Separate-value storage describes a vault/reference model, not every cryptographic or irreversible tokenization design. Token vault authorization, mapping protection, keys and retention need independent validation. Preserve prior m648 qualified lookup distinction and do not equate tokenization with hashing, encryption or steganography. No lookup, payment or vault test occurred.',
 'HOLD hardware chip as mandatory discrete packaging: discrete, integrated and firmware TPM implementations exist. TPM object attributes, authorization and duplication policy govern key use/export; not every key is universally nonexportable. Measurements record evidence; quotes need fresh verifier checks, a trusted baseline and relevant coverage. Measured boot is not Secure Boot enforcement or proof of malware-free runtime. TPM platform custody differs from HSM and enclave roles; their names do not establish validated custody. Preserve prior TPM-card/m622/p018 distinctions. No module, key, quote or boot policy was checked.'
];
